INT. NIGHT-SHIFT BUS DEPOT - CANTEEN - NIGHT

Steel tables. A buzzing vending machine. Three drivers on break.

HILARY NWOSU-ABRAMS (50s, thermos) stirs her coffee. WALTER "WALT" GRZESKOWIAK (60s, reading glasses) does a crossword. And newcomer DARNELL FAIRWEATHER (20s, new uniform) sits stiffly, hands folded.

                    WALT
          Seven letters. "Bus stop's neighbor."

                    HILARY
          Shelter.

                    WALT
          That's seven. Thanks.

                    DARNELL
          (to the group)
          Is it always this quiet?

Hilary shrugs. Walt doesn't look up.

                    DARNELL (CONT'D)
          I mean, I thought there'd be more people.

                    HILARY
          People go home, honey.

                    DARNELL
          Right.

He rubs his palms on his knees. She watches him over the rim of her cup.

                    HILARY
          First night?

                    DARNELL
          Third. But the first two I was shadowing.

Walt fills in a word. Hilary taps the table twice.

                    HILARY
          (to Walt, low)
          He's going to ask.

                    WALT
          (to himself)
          Six down. "Lonely."

                    DARNELL
          Ask what?

Hilary smiles and slides her thermos across the table. He pours a little, hesitates, and sips. She nods. Walt finally looks up and gives him the pencil.

                    WALT
          Do you know a four-letter word for "home"?

Darnell takes the pencil and studies the grid. The vending machine hums in the quiet.

                    DARNELL
          (quietly)
          Nest.

Walt writes it in. He pushes the paper to the middle of the table, and the three of them lean over it together.
